  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Vallon Sourn ("dark" in Provençal) is a small 3 km gorge carved by the Argens in the limestone plateau, between Châteauvert and Correns, in the Provence Verte. It is a protected natural site: walking, swimming, picnics, climbing and canoeing. From the car park, an 800 m walk leads to the banks and their beaches.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Note: the site is closed. The commune of Correns writes, in a news item of 27/08/2026, that the Vallon Sourn and the road running through it remain closed to traffic, "all means included: vehicle, bike, pedestrian, boat", until the safety works are carried out. The Provence Verte & Verdon tourist office reports it as "inaccessible following this summer's fires" and "temporarily closed" (listing updated on 14/09/2026). The local press (Fréquence Sud, 22/10/2026) reports that the Var Département, which manages this natural area, speaks of several months of works, because of the risk of rockfalls and mudslides on the RD45. The information below describes the site as the tourist office presents it in normal times.</p>
      <h2>The rule for dogs</h2>
      <p>When the site is open, the rules posted by the tourist office ask that dogs be kept on a leash, and the listing says "Pets accepted". It also bans hunting and fishing, fires, camping, picking plants and motor vehicles; flying drones is banned for recreational pilots. Some places, notably the south bank, are off-limits to protect the habitat.</p>
      <h2>On site, in normal times</h2>
      <p>The valley is a small 3 km gorge carved by the Argens in the limestone plateau; "sourn" means "dark" in Provençal. The cliffs, up to 40 m high, shade the river. It is a 230-hectare Sensitive Natural Area of the Var Département, classified Natura 2000, a refuge for wildlife and plants. From the car park, an 800 m walk leads to the banks of the Argens and its two wild beaches. Swimming is unsupervised and at users' own risk. The free 43-space car park is at Mas de Bagarèdes, on the D45, 1.5 km from Châteauvert, with no parking from 9pm to 7am and a height limit of 2.10 m; the main entrance and the first signs are on the other side of the road.</p>
      <p>A disabled parking space in the heart of the valley gives access to the main beach by a boardwalk. There are dry toilets at the foot of the cliffs and near the beach, picnic tables by the beach and an emergency call point at the foot of the cliff; there is no mobile signal on site. Remember to bring water for your dog. From June to September, access to forest massifs is regulated by the prefecture because of the fire risk (map published every evening at 6pm), and the D45 is closed when the massifs are.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: do not try to get into the valley until Correns town hall or the tourist office announces it has reopened. Numbers published by the tourist office: 04 94 04 61 87 (Cotignac and Correns), 04 94 77 20 01 (Barjols and Châteauvert).</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The tourist office mentions a guided walk from April to October, by reservation, to explore the valley, as well as a canoe rental provider in Correns. These activities depend on the site reopening.</p>
